### ============================================================
### 7. AUTHENTICATION NOTES
### ============================================================

# After logging in, extract the token from the response and set @auth_token
# Most endpoints require : Authorization: Bearer {token}
# 
# User Roles:
# - CUSTOMER: Create/manage their own shipments, view tracking
# - ADMIN: Access all admin endpoints, manage users, resolve issues
#
# Protected Routes:
# - Auth Service: /admin/* (ADMIN only)
# - Admin Service: ALL endpoints (ADMIN only)
# - Shipment Service: /admin/* (ADMIN only), POST/PUT/DELETE (CUSTOMER own)
# - Tracking Service: /documents/upload (ADMIN only)

### ============================================================
### 8. STATUS VALUES & TRANSITIONS
### ============================================================

# Valid Shipment Status Values:
# - DRAFT: Initial state after creation
# - BOOKED: Confirmed ready for pickup
# - PICKED_UP: Collected from sender
# - IN_TRANSIT: In the delivery network
# - OUT_FOR_DELIVERY: With delivery agent
# - DELIVERED: Successfully delivered
#
# Status Transition Rules:
# DRAFT -> BOOKED (Book Shipment endpoint)
# BOOKED -> PICKED_UP -> IN_TRANSIT -> OUT_FOR_DELIVERY -> DELIVERED
# DRAFT/BOOKED -> CANCELLED (Cancel Shipment endpoint)

### ============================================================
### 9. ERROR CODES & MEANINGS
### ============================================================

# 200 OK - Request successful
# 201 Created - Resource created successfully
# 400 Bad Request - Invalid input parameters
# 401 Unauthorized - Missing or invalid token
# 403 Forbidden - Insufficient permissions for resource
# 404 Not Found - Resource not found
# 500 Internal Server Error - Server error

### ============================================================
### 11. GATEWAY vs DIRECT SERVICE - ENDPOINT MAPPING
### ============================================================

# GATEWAY URL PATTERN              DIRECT SERVICE URL PATTERN
# 
# Auth Service Routes:
# POST {{gateway}}/auth/login       POST {{auth_service}}/api/auth/login
# POST {{gateway}}/auth/signup      POST {{auth_service}}/api/auth/signup
# GET {{gateway}}/auth/admin/users  GET {{auth_service}}/api/auth/admin/users
#
# Admin Service Routes:
# GET {{gateway}}/admin/dashboard   GET {{admin_service}}/api/admin/dashboard
# GET {{gateway}}/admin/shipments   GET {{admin_service}}/api/admin/shipments
# PUT {{gateway}}/admin/users/{id}  PUT {{admin_service}}/api/admin/users/{id}
#
# Shipment Service Routes:
# POST {{gateway}}/shipments        POST {{shipment_service}}/api/shipments
# GET {{gateway}}/shipments/my      GET {{shipment_service}}/api/shipments/my
# PUT {{gateway}}/shipments/{id}    PUT {{shipment_service}}/api/shipments/{id}
#
# Tracking Service Routes:
# GET {{gateway}}/tracking/{id}     GET {{tracking_service}}/api/tracking/{id}
# POST {{gateway}}/tracking/{id}/status  PUT {{tracking_service}}/api/tracking/{id}/status

### ============================================================
### 12. GATEWAY ADVANTAGES & USE CASES
### ============================================================

# ✅ WHY USE GATEWAY ENDPOINTS:
#
# 1. UNIFIED ENTRY POINT
#    - Single URL for all microservices
#    - Client doesn't need to know individual service ports
#    - Easier load balancing
#
# 2. CENTRALIZED AUTHENTICATION
#    - JWT validation at gateway level
#    - Reduced auth logic in individual services
#    - Single point for token refresh
#
# 3. CROSS-CUTTING CONCERNS
#    - Logging at gateway level
#    - Rate limiting enforcement
#    - Request/response transformation
#
# 4. SERVICE DISCOVERY
#    - Services can change ports without client updates
#    - Gateway configuration centralized (ocelot.json)
#
# 5. API VERSIONING
#    - Easy to version entire API
#    - Can deprecate legacy endpoints gracefully
#
# 6. PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT
#    - Services behind gateway not directly exposed
#    - Better security posture
#    - Easier to implement WAF (Web Application Firewall)

### ============================================================
### 13. TESTING WORKFLOW - GATEWAY FIRST APPROACH
### ============================================================

# RECOMMENDED TESTING ORDER:
#
# 1. Direct Service Test (Verify service works independently)
#    - Test: POST {{auth_service}}/api/auth/login
#    - Purpose: Ensure service is running
#
# 2. Gateway Test (Verify gateway routing)
#    - Test: POST {{gateway}}/auth/login
#    - Purpose: Ensure Ocelot routing is working
#
# 3. Authentication Flow (Test token generation)
#    - Test: POST {{gateway}}/auth/login
#    - Extract token and save to @auth_token
#
# 4. Protected Routes (Test authorization)
#    - Test: GET {{gateway}}/admin/dashboard
#    - Header: Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
#    - Purpose: Verify JWT validation at gateway
#
# 5. Full Workflow (Integration test)
#    - Test: Complete workflow from Steps 1-10 using gateway URLs

### ============================================================
### 14. COMMON GATEWAY TESTING SCENARIOS
### ============================================================

# SCENARIO 1: Test Gateway Routing
# - Hit Gateway endpoint
# - Verify response matches direct service response
# - Check Ocelot routing logs
#
# SCENARIO 2: Test Token Propagation
# - Get token via Gateway
# - Use token on protected Gateway endpoint
# - Verify authorization works across gateway
#
# SCENARIO 3: Test Cross-Service Workflows
# - Create Shipment via Gateway (goes to Shipment Service)
# - Track Shipment via Gateway (goes to Tracking Service)
# - View Shipment in Admin via Gateway (goes to Admin Service)
# - Verify all services have consistent data
#
# SCENARIO 4: Test Gateway Failure Scenarios
# - Stop one microservice
# - Try hitting that service through Gateway
# - Verify gateway returns appropriate error
# - Restart service and verify recovery
#
# SCENARIO 5: Performance Testing
# - Compare response time direct service vs gateway
# - Measure gateway overhead
# - Identify bottlenecks

